+<main id="main"><div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/">Buy</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Land</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Buying land</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Land for sale in Horry County, SC<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">What an acre costs, and what you can build on it.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 14, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">An acre of land for sale is listed at about $25,000 around Loris and about $150,000 near Myrtle Beach. A lot with no sewer needs a state septic permit first.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Land for sale in Horry County is listed at about $25,000 an acre around Loris and $40,000 around Aynor. In Myrtle Beach, Carolina Forest and Socastee it is about $150,000. Those are tracts of an acre or more, and the bigger the tract, the less each acre costs.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Before you offer, three things matter most: the state's septic permit, the zoning and the flood zone. We check all three for you.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bot