om:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)}main#main div[style^="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0"] td{border:0!important;padding:.1rem 0!important}main#main div[style^="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0"] td:first-child{font-weight:650;color:var(--navy)}main#main div[style^="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0"] td[data-l]::before{content:attr(data-l) ": ";color:var(--muted);font-weight:500}}main#main .c3-gchart{max-width:720px!important;box-sizing:border-box}</style><p><a class="tb-go" href="https://horrycountytreasurer.qpaybill.com/Taxes/TaxesDefaultType4.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pay on the county's tax site<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M14 4h6v6M20 4l-9 9M18 14v5a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H5a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V7a1 1 0 0 1 1-1h5"/></svg></a></p><p class="tb-go-note">The site belongs to the Horry County Treasurer. Chapter3 is a real estate brokerage, not the county. Use Chrome or Edge: payments do not go through in Safari.</p><ol class="tb-steps"><li><span class="ic"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="30" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span><div><h3>Pick the bill type and the year.</h3><p>Choose Real Estate for a house, condo or lot, or Vehicle for a car or truck. The search holds about ten years of bills, paid and unpaid.</p></div></li><li><span class="ic"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="30" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><div><h3>Search by owner name, PIN, map number or notice number.</h3><p>Type the owner's last name first. There is no address search. The PIN is on the parcel card of the <a href="/buyers/property-records/gis-map/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">county GIS map</a>.</p></div></li><li><span class="ic"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="30" height="30" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><div><h3>Open the bill and pay it.</h3><p>Pay by e-check or card. The county mails a receipt, and you can print a copy from the same site.</p></div></li></ol><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Have only an address? We find the bill.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Give us the address. We find the PIN and last year's bill, and send you both.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us find the bill</a></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Deeds, the parcel map and the county's other records are in our <a href="/buyers/property-records/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Horry County property records guide</a>. To find a deed, see our <a href="/buyers/property-records/deeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">deed search guide</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="fees"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Fees">How much does it cost to pay Horry County taxes online?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Paying online by e-check costs nothing; a credit or debit card costs 2.35%.</p><div class="tb-t" style="margin:1.1rem 0 1.3rem;max-width:720px;overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">How you pay</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">No card</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Card</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Online</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">E-check: free</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">2.35%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">By phone</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">E-check: $0.50</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">2.35% plus $0.50</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">In person</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Check or cash</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Credit card: 2.35%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">By mail or drop box</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Check or money order</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Not taken</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class="tb-list"><li><strong>The oldest year comes first.</strong> While any older tax is unpaid, the county will not take this year's bill online or by phone. The oldest year must be paid first.</li><li><strong>The county keeps none of the fee.</strong> The card fee goes to the payment company. Visa, Mastercard and Discover are taken.</li><li><strong>By mail,</strong> send a check or money order to the Horry County Treasurer, with the notice number written on the check.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="due"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Due dates">When are Horry County property taxes due?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County property taxes are due January 15, for the bill mailed in October. A mailed payment counts by its postmark.</p><figure class="tb-fig tb-small"><div class="tb-card tb-pen"><div class="hd"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg><span>Pay by January 15 and there is no penalty</span></div><svg viewBox="0 0 640 330" role="img" aria-label="The penalty on an unpaid Horry County property tax bill: none through January 15, 3% from January 16, 10% in all from February 2, and 15% in all from March 17, when the tax is delinquent."><line x1="0" y1="236" x2="640" y2="236" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.25)" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="32" y="231" width="96" height="5" rx="2.5" fill="#2f6f8f"/><text class="v" x="80" y="215" text-anchor="middle">None</text><text class="d" x="80" y="278" text-anchor="middle">January</text><text class="d" x="80" y="316" text-anchor="middle">15</text><rect x="192" y="204" width="96" height="32" rx="10" fill="#c4783a"/><text class="v" x="240" y="188" text-anchor="middle">3%</text><text class="d" x="240" y="278" text-anchor="middle">January</text><text class="d" x="240" y="316" text-anchor="middle">16</text><rect x="352" y="129" width="96" height="107" rx="10" fill="#c4783a"/><text class="v" x="400" y="113" text-anchor="middle">10%</text><text class="d" x="400" y="278" text-anchor="middle">February</text><text class="d" x="400" y="316" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="512" y="76" width="96" height="160" rx="10" fill="#91592b"/><text class="v" x="560" y="60" text-anchor="middle">15%</text><text class="d" x="560" y="278" text-anchor="middle">March</text><text class="d" x="560" y="316" text-anchor="middle">17</text></svg></div><figcaption>Each penalty is added to the unpaid tax on the date shown. On March 17 the tax is delinquent.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not getting the bill does not stop the penalty.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Did my lender already pay it?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If your monthly mortgage payment includes the tax, held in an escrow account, the mortgage company may pay the county before January 15. The tax search shows each bill as paid or unpaid, with the date it was paid.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Bought this year, and the bill went to the seller?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a home changes hands during the year, the bill can go to the prior owner. If the new owner got no notice in time, state law requires the Treasurer to waive the penalty. The tax itself is still owed. At closing, the <a href="/buyers/closing-costs/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">seller credits you for their share of the year</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="installments"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Installments">Can you pay Horry County property taxes in installments?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Horry County takes five payments ahead, each 16.67% of last year's tax, then mails a final bill for the rest.</p><div class="tb-t" style="margin:1.1rem 0 1.3rem;max-width:720px;overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Payment</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Due</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Payment 1</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">February 15</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Payment 2</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">April 15</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Payment 3</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">June 15</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Payment 4</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">August 15</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Payment 5</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">October 15</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Final bill, for the rest</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Mailed by November 15, due January 15</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class="tb-list"><li><strong>Sign up between December 1 and January 15,</strong> with one form for each parcel. The <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/installment-payments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">county's installment page</a> has the form.</li><li><strong>Real property only:</strong> land, buildings and mobile homes. Not vehicles, boats, or a tax a lender pays from escrow.</li><li><strong>Pay with the coupons</strong> in person, by mail or in a drop box. Installments cannot be paid online.</li><li><strong>The county holds the money until the last payment,</strong> then applies it to the bill. An owner who sells asks the Treasurer for a refund in writing, because a buyer cannot use the seller's payments.</li><li><strong>The packet comes again each year</strong> until you opt out in writing. An application can be turned down when older taxes are unpaid, the tax is escrowed, or the applicant is not the owner.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="search"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Search tax records">How do you search Horry County tax records by name, PIN or address?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The county's tax search finds a bill by owner name, PIN, map number, notice number or tax ID. It has no address search.</p><ul class="tb-list"><li><strong>Owner name:</strong> last name first, then the first name.</li><li><strong>PIN:</strong> the 11-digit number every county office uses for a parcel. It is on the parcel card of the county map.</li><li><strong>Map number:</strong> the 10-digit tax map number, on the same card and on the bill.</li><li><strong>Notice number:</strong> printed on the bill. It finds that one bill.</li><li><strong>Only an address?</strong> The county sends address searches to its Land Records map, which needs a county account. Our <a href="/buyers/property-records/gis-map/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">GIS map guide</a> shows how to get in and find the PIN.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Open a bill from the results to see its lines. The search needs no sign-in.</p><figure class="tb-fig"><div class="tb-card" role="img" aria-label="A drawing of the lines on a Horry County real estate tax bill, with a plain meaning beside each: total appraisal, total assessed, the district and city levy, map number and PIN, county tax, city tax and fees, the exemptions and credits, and total taxes."><div class="hd"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg><span>Real estate tax bill</span></div><dl><div class="r"><dt>Total Appraisal</dt><dd>The Assessor's value of the land and buildings.</dd></div><div class="r"><dt>Total Assessed</dt><dd>The taxable value: 4% of the appraisal on a home you live in, 6% on most other property.</dd></div><div class="r"><dt>District/Levy, City/Levy</dt><dd>The tax district, and the town if the property is inside one.</dd></div><div class="r"><dt>Map Number, PIN</dt><dd>The two numbers to search the bill again next year.</dd></div><div class="r"><dt>County Tax, City Tax, Fees</dt><dd>Each tax and fee on the property.</dd></div><div class="r"><dt>Exemptions, Local Option Credit</dt><dd>The credits that lower the bill.</dd></div><div class="r k"><dt>Total Taxes</dt><dd>What is owed for the year.</dd></div></dl></div><figcaption>A drawing of the lines on the county's bill page, with what each one means. No real bill is shown.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The 4% rate on a home you live in</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home you live in, which the county calls a legal residence, is taxed on 4% of its value. It also gets a credit that removes the school operating tax. The owner applies to the Assessor. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Horry County property tax guide</a> shows how to apply and what the tax on a home will be.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Paying the 6% rate on a home you live in is the costliest <a href="/buyers/common-mistakes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">common buyer mistake</a> here. For the full yearly cost of a home, tax included, see <a href="/buyers/cost-to-own/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what it costs to own a home here</a>.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Looking at a house? See its tax bill before you offer.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We read last year's bill for that house and tell you what changes when you buy it.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us pull the bill</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="assessor"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Assessor and tax offices">What does the Horry County Tax Assessor do?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Horry County Assessor values the land and buildings on the bill. The Assessor also reviews the applications for the 4% rate on a home you live in. The Auditor figures the bill, and the Treasurer takes the payment.</p><div class="tb-offices" role="list" aria-label="Three county offices, three jobs"><div class="tb-card" role="listitem"><div class="hd"><span class="ic"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span><span>Assessor</span></div><ul><li>Values the land and buildings</li><li>Keeps who owns what</li><li>Reviews applications for the 4% rate</li><li>Takes address changes</li><li>In Conway and at the Central Coast Complex</li><li><a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/assessor/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Assessor's page</a></li></ul><p class="ph"><a href="tel:+18439155040" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;white-space:nowrap">(843) 915-5040</a></p></div><div class="tb-card" role="listitem"><div class="hd"><span class="ic"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span><span>Auditor</span></div><ul><li>Figures each bill</li><li>Writes the vehicle tax bills</li></ul><p class="ph"><a href="tel:+18439155050" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;white-space:nowrap">(843) 915-5050</a></p></div><div class="tb-card" role="listitem"><div class="hd"><span class="ic"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><span>Treasurer</span></div><ul><li>Collects every tax payment</li><li>Keeps the receipts</li><li>Runs the installment plan and the tax sale</li></ul><p class="ph"><a href="tel:+18439155470" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;white-space:nowrap">(843) 915-5470</a></p></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Assessor does not take payments. Every payment goes to the Treasurer, online or at one of its offices.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where are the Horry County tax offices?</h3><div class="tb-t" style="margin:1.1rem 0 1.3rem;max-width:720px;overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Treasurer's office</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Address</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Conway, the main office</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">1301 Second Avenue, Conway</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Myrtle Beach</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">332 Hinson Drive, in the Central Coast Complex</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">South Strand</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">9630 Scipio Lane, Surfside</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Stephen's Crossroads</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">107 Highway 57 North, Little River</td></tr></tbody></table></div><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="max-width:720px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/hoa-liens-and-foreclosure/horry-government-justice-center-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px" width="1200" height="675" alt="The Horry County Government and Justice Center in Conway: brick wings on each side of a white columned entrance, seen across the parking lot" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia</a></span></div><figcaption>The Horry County Government and Justice Center at 1301 Second Avenue in Conway. The Treasurer's main office is here, and so is the tax sale each December.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each office is open 8 to 5 on weekdays. The Myrtle Beach office is now in the county's new <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/news/articles/horry-county-marks-opening-of-central-coast-complex-with-ribbon-cutting/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Central Coast Complex</a> in <a href="/submarkets/carolina-forest/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Carolina Forest</a>. The Treasurer, the Auditor and the Assessor share the building. Mail goes to P.O. Box 1237, Conway, SC 29528.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="appeal"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Appeal a value">How do you appeal your Horry County property value?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Write to the Horry County Assessor within 90 days after a value notice is mailed. The Assessor mails a notice when a value rises by $1,000 or more.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a year with no notice, you may appeal in writing at any time. In that year, one filed by January 15 counts for that year's bill, and a later one counts for the next year.</p><ol class="tb-steps"><li><span class="no" aria-hidden="true">1</span><div><h3>Object in writing.</h3><p>Use the Property Appeal form on the county's <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tax Payer Services page</a>.</p></div></li><li><span class="no" aria-hidden="true">2</span><div><h3>Meet the Assessor.</h3><p>The Assessor schedules a meeting within <strong>30 days</strong> of your request.</p></div></li><li><span class="no" aria-hidden="true">3</span><div><h3>File a written protest.</h3><p>If the meeting does not settle it, you have <strong>30 days</strong> to protest in writing, with the facts, your reasons and the value you think is right.</p></div></li><li><span class="no" aria-hidden="true">4</span><div><h3>Go to the Board of Assessment Appeals.</h3><p>You have <strong>30 days</strong> after the Assessor's written answer.</p></div></li><li><span class="no" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div><h3>Ask for a hearing at the Administrative Law Court.</h3><p>You have <strong>30 days</strong> after the board's written decision.</p></div></li></ol><ul class="tb-list"><li><strong>The bill still comes.</strong> If an appeal will not be settled by December 31, the bill is figured on 80% of the protested assessment. The difference is billed or refunded, with interest, after the decision.</li><li><strong>Values reset every fifth year.</strong> The last countywide reset took effect for the 2024 tax year. Between resets, reassessment alone can raise a property's taxable value by at most 15% over five years.</li><li><strong>The 15% cap does not cover an addition or the year after a sale.</strong> Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax guide</a> shows what a sale means for a buyer's tax.</li><li><strong>Who can present it:</strong> you, a lawyer, a CPA, an enrolled agent, or a licensed or certified appraiser on a value question. An enrolled agent is a tax professional the IRS approves to represent taxpayers. A real estate agent cannot.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="unpaid"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Unpaid tax">What happens if Horry County property tax is not paid?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County property tax still unpaid on March 17 is delinquent. After a series of notices, the property goes to the county's tax sale in early December.</p><figure class="tb-fig"><div class="tb-card tb-tl" role="img" aria-label="After March 17: a delinquent notice about April 1, a certified notice 30 days later, a notice posted on the property between May and October that it is seized for sale, three weeks of newspaper ads, the tax sale in early December, and 12 months for the owner to pay and get it back."><div class="hd"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg><span>From a missed bill to the tax sale</span></div><ol><li class=""><span class="d">April 1 or soon after</span><span class="w">The county mails a delinquent notice.</span></li><li class=""><span class="d">30 days later</span><span class="w">A certified notice follows.</span></li><li class=""><span class="d">May to October</span><span class="w">A notice is posted on the property: seized, to be sold for the tax.</span></li><li class=""><span class="d">Three Thursdays before the sale</span><span class="w">The property is advertised in the newspaper.</span></li><li class="end"><span class="d">Early December</span><span class="w">The tax sale, at the county building in Conway.</span></li><li class=""><span class="d">12 months after the sale</span><span class="w">The last day the owner can pay the tax and costs and get the property back.</span></li></ol></div></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The opening bid at the sale is all the unpaid tax, penalties and costs, plus this year's tax.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Nearly half the properties on the tax sale list have an opening bid under $2,500"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Nearly half the properties on the tax sale list have an opening bid under $2,500</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Opening bid</span><span role="columnheader">Properties</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Under $500</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.243" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">117</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$500 to $999</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.509" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">245</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$1,000 to $2,499</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">481</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$2,500 to $4,999</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.954" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">459</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$5,000 to $9,999</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.661" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">318</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$10,000 to $24,999</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.252" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">121</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">$25,000 or more</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.064" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our count of the opening bids on the county's list, by range. The three shaded ranges, under $2,500, hold 843 properties.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The county's list of November 12 holds 1,772 properties, about 1 parcel in 170 in the county. A second list holds 459 mobile homes without land. Both lists get shorter as owners pay.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Buying at the sale has its own rules on deposits, bidding and the title. Our <a href="/invest/foreclosures/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">foreclosure and tax sale guide</a> covers them.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="vehicle"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Vehicle tax">How do you pay Horry County vehicle tax?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Pay Horry County vehicle tax on the same county site. The tax is paid a year ahead, before the DMV renews or issues the registration.</p><ul class="tb-list"><li><strong>Renewals.</strong> The bill comes about a month before the plate expires. Pay it online or by phone.</li><li><strong>The high mileage discount</strong> is only given in the month the tax is due.</li><li><strong>A first registration needs the paper receipt.</strong> The DMV does not take the receipt printed after an online payment, so get the original at a Treasurer's office.</li><li><strong>A car bought from a dealer</strong> gets a bill that is due when it comes. If it is still unpaid 120 days after the purchase, a final notice gives 30 more days before the driver's license and registration are suspended.</li><li><strong>New to the area?</strong> The order of the steps is in our <a href="/buyers/relocating/moving-checklist/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">moving checklist</a>.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If a property was not the home you lived in on December 31, the Auditor asks for a personal property return (PT-100). Furniture in a rental is taxed on its own notice; our <a href="/invest/str-setup/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental setup guide</a> covers it.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="georgetown"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Georgetown County">Georgetown County tax payments for Pawleys Island and Litchfield</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="/submarkets/pawleys-island/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Pawleys Island</a> and Litchfield are in Georgetown County, which takes tax payments online at <a href="https://georgetowncountysctax.com/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">georgetowncountysctax.com</a>.</p><ul class="tb-list"><li><strong>Card or e-check online,</strong> through a payment company that charges its own fee. The county keeps none of it.</li><li><strong>The <a href="https://gtcountysc.gov/288/Treasurer" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Treasurer</a></strong> is at 129 Screven Street in Georgetown, <a href="tel:+18435453101" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;white-space:nowrap">(843) 545-3101</a>, open 8:30 to 5 on weekdays.</li><li><strong>The DMV does not take the online receipt.</strong> The county mails an official receipt within three business days.</li><li><strong>Installments,</strong> five payments on last year's tax, but you <a href="https://gtcountysc.gov/DocumentCenter/View/456/Application-for-Advance-Installment-Payments-PDF" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">apply every year</a> by January 15, with last year's tax paid.</li><li><strong>Unpaid on March 17,</strong> the tax is delinquent, and the tax sale is in December.</li><li><strong>Values were reset countywide for 2025.</strong> You have 90 days from a <a href="https://gtcountysc.gov/268/Reassessment" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">reassessment notice</a> to appeal, free, on the form at the bottom of the notice. The Assessor is <a href="tel:+18435453017" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;white-space:nowrap">(843) 545-3017</a>.</li><li><strong>A rental gets two notices,</strong> one for the property and one for its furniture, fixtures and equipment.</li><li><strong>Property records</strong> are on the Assessor's <a href="https://www.qpublic.net/sc/georgetown/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">qPublic site</a>.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="horry-county-tax-payment-questions"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Horry County tax payment questions</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I pay Horry County taxes by phone?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, for a small fee, shown in the fee table above. For a question about a bill, the Treasurer's main line is (843) 915-5470.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How do I get a copy of my Horry County tax receipt?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Print it from the Tax Payments and Receipts site: search the bill, then open it. A printed copy will not do for a car's first registration.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">I never got a tax bill. Am I still late?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. The 3% penalty starts January 16 whether or not a bill arrived. The one exception is a home bought that year whose bill went to the seller.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a Horry County tax penalty be waived?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, in two cases. One is a home bought this year whose bill went to the seller. The other is a written request with clear proof you mailed the payment on time; the Treasurer's answer to it is final.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is there unclaimed tax money in my name?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The county keeps a search of unclaimed tax refunds, by name or notice number, on its Tax Payer Services page. A claim takes 3 to 6 weeks. Refunds dated before 2022 are with the State Treasurer.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where is the Horry County tax office in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At 332 Hinson Drive in Carolina Forest, inside the county's Central Coast Complex.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is Chapter3 part of Horry County?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Chapter3 is a real estate brokerage. The tax site, the bill and the payment belong to the Horry County Treasurer. We find a bill or pull it for buyers and owners, free.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://horrycountytreasurer.qpaybill.com/Taxes/TaxesDefaultType4.aspx" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tax Payments and Receipts</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tax Payer Services</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/real-property-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Real Property Tax</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/installment-payments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Installment Payments</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/treasurer/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Treasurer</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/treasurer/delinquent-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Delinquent Tax</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/questioning-values/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Questioning Values</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/assessor/guide-to-assessment/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Guide to Assessment</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/vehicle-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vehicle Tax</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c045.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-45</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c051.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">12-51</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c060.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">12-60</a>, <a href="https://gtcountysc.gov/288/Treasurer" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgetown Treasurer</a>. Our count: the Treasurer's tax sale lists as of November 12, opening bids only, no owner or address. Not legal or tax advice.</p><style>.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">The Horry County Government and Justice Center at 1301 Second Avenue in Conway. The Treasurer's main office is here, and so is the tax sale each December. Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. C