+<main id="main"><div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/relocating/">Relocating</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Myrtle Beach vs Charleston</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Moving to Myrtle Beach</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Myrtle Beach or Charleston?<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">More home for the money here. More jobs, flights and history in Charleston.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 14, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Myrtle Beach and Charleston side by side: home prices, property tax, the beach, the summer, flood insurance, jobs and the drive to work.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The typical home in the Myrtle Beach area is worth about $340,000, about $98,000 less than in the Charleston area. In Myrtle Beach, after the summer season, you can walk a leashed dog on the beach at any hour.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Charleston has more jobs, higher pay and a busier airport, and ferries leave downtown for Fort Sumter, where the Civil War began. The saving here goes furthest when your income or savings come with you.</p><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:2rem;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transfo