 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I get a CLUE report on a house I want to buy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Only the owner, an insurer or a lender can order the CLUE report on a house, and it covers only recent years. We ask the seller to order it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I see FEMA flood claims by address?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not until you own the house. Once you do, you can ask FEMA for its claims, including those from before you owned it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a severe repetitive loss property?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Severe repetitive loss is FEMA's label for a house that has flooded again and again. FEMA uses it for four or more paid flood claims over $5,000 each, or for building payments that add up to more than the house is worth, with two of them within 10 years of each other. The label stays with the address when the house sells."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will the lender tell me if the house is in a flood zone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, if the house is in FEMA's high-risk zone and the loan is from a bank or other federally regulated lender. The lender must make sure you are told in writing a reasonable time before you sign. The notice is about the zone, not about past floods."}}]}</script>
