+<main id="main"><div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/">Buyers</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Waterfront homes</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Waterfront homes</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Waterfront homes in Myrtle Beach:<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">from the ocean to the lake, what each one costs.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 14, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">From the sun coming up out of the ocean to a boat at your own dock, seven kinds of water, each with its own price.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the past year, a house on the beach sold for about $2.2 million at the middle, and one on the Intracoastal Waterway for about $1.3 million.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A condo in an oceanfront building sold for about $200,000, and every waterfront house for sale under $300,000 is on a lake or pond.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Tell us the boat and the budget. We send the homes on the water that fit.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Send