 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is South Carolina a 50/50 divorce state?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The law has no equal-split rule, so the judge's split of the house can be more or less than half."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays the mortgage during the divorce?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Both of you owe the lender while both names are on the loan, so a missed payment is missed for both of you. While the case is open, the Family Court can order temporary support. A support order can make one spouse pay debts for the other, such as the mortgage."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if we owe more than the house is worth?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Then the money from a sale would not pay off the loan. The lender may agree to a short sale, a sale for less than what is owed. If the lender forgives the rest, get that in writing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a house I inherited marital property in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually not. What one spouse inherited is not marital property under the law. Whether it became marital, for example after it was put in both names, is a question for a family law attorney."}}]}</script>
